// `@entry`: the entry point chosen by annotation instead of by the name `main`
// (Entry Points §E.2, ERRATA E105).
//
// A program written for a platform that names its own entry, or one whose
// author simply wants the first function to be called `launch`, marks it
// `@entry`. The annotation replaces the `main` rule rather than overriding it:
// a binary carrying both an `@entry` and a `main` is `E0320`, and a second
// `@entry` is `E0321`.
//
// Everything `main` gets, an `@entry` function gets, because the runtime is
// handing the same things to the same shape of function: the command line
// without the program name, and an `int` return taken as the process exit code.
// `@entry(symbol = "...")` additionally publishes the function to the linker
// under that name, beside the entry the C runtime starts.

/** Where the work of this example lives, called like any other function. */
int summarize(String[] args) {
    print($"arguments: ${args.length}");
    return args.length;
}

@entry(symbol = "jux_demo_start")
public int launch(String[] args) {
    print("launch: the entry point is not called main");
    var extra = summarize(args);
    // Calling the entry point by its Jux name is ordinary too: the annotation
    // publishes a symbol and selects the entry, it does not reserve the name.
    print($"exiting with ${extra}");
    return extra;
}
